# Proposal: a reply as written

You are shown the documented rules of the checks, not their code. Write your input as a REPLY a language model would actually give when asked to fill a form from a document: a heading, a sentence of reasoning or hedging, perhaps a quoted draft it then corrects, the form as JSON, perhaps a code fence with a label line inside it, perhaps a closing remark or a second copy of the form. The rewritten text is the same reply with ONE edit a real model might make: a label line inside the fence, a note between two copies, a sentence quoting the draft, an apology before the form, a trailing summary that repeats a field. Say what the rule, read as written, says the check's answer ought to do. Your kernel is one of conformance.kernel.recovery, conformance.kernel.recovered-from-prose, conformance.kernel.response-verdict, conformance.kernel.refusal-phrase. The commitments are a STRING whose content is JSON of the form {"kernel": "<id>", "input": "<the input text>", "rewritten": "<your rewritten text>", "expect": "moves" or "unchanged", "rewrite": "<one sentence naming what you changed>"}. The input and the rewritten text are instances written out in full, never descriptions. Do not repeat a pair shown among the earlier proposals.

## The problem (problem)
The checks under test are the conformance harness's own functions. Most seats are shown only their documented rules, not their code: how the harness recovers a JSON object from a reply, whether it had to recover it from prose, its response verdict, its refusal-phrase check, and its grounding matcher. The question: is there an input and a rewrite of it on which the rule, read as written, says one thing and the check, run by machine, does another? The executor runs the check on both texts and records what its answer did; nothing here decides anything, the record shows where a reading and the machine parted.

